Key observations:
- signal_history stores Latitude/Longitude as separate float64 signals (Location map is skipped)
- Temperature fields are written by both CompleteWithTx (as params) and PartialUpdateWithTx (via enhancedFields)
- PartialUpdateWithTx runs AFTER CompleteWithTx in the transaction, so enhancedFields can overwrite NULL temps
- SnapshotAt returns nil map on error; Go nil map reads are safe (return zero, false)

=== REASONING ===

Problem: When a drive ends, enhancedFields are built from session accumulators (speed, temp,
position collected during the drive). If Tesla delta encoding means a signal wasn't sent during
the drive (e.g., temp unchanged), those fields are nil/zero.

Solution: Use SnapshotAt() from signal_history as a fallback to reconstruct signal state at
drive start/end times. Three changes needed:

1. Add signalHistoryWriter field to TelemetrySessionTracker struct
2. Forward signalHistoryWriter from TelemetryHandler.SetSignalHistoryWriter to sessionTracker
3. After existing enhancedFields construction, enrich missing fields from signal_history snapshots

Fields enriched (all in drivePartialAllowed):
- start_lat, start_lon ← startSnapshot["Latitude"], startSnapshot["Longitude"]
- end_lat, end_lon ← endSnapshot["Latitude"], endSnapshot["Longitude"]
- inside_temp_avg_c ← startSnapshot["InsideTemp"] (when insideAvg is nil)
- outside_temp_avg_c ← startSnapshot["OutsideTemp"] (when outsideAvg is nil)

Constraints satisfied:
- Existing enhancedFields construction preserved (primary path)
- signal_history is fallback only (_, ok check before each field)
- SnapshotAt errors are non-fatal (log.Warn and continue)
- signal_history reads happen BEFORE the transaction boundary
- Tesla signal names mapped to DB column names

